<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Llucmajor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">25 October <a href="/years/1349">1349</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/october-25">On this day: 25 October</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Llucmajor was fought on 25 October 1349 near Llucmajor in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>. James III, the deposed king of Majorca, landed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> to retake his kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a>, but was defeated and killed by the Aragonese army. His death ended all hope of restoring the independent Kingdom of Majorca.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-llucmajor.jpg" alt="Battle of Llucmajor" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoni Salvà · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rei_Jaume_III_Llucmajor_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-majorca">James III of Majorca</a> held his kingdom as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV of Aragon</a>, and from 1341 Peter gathered legal grievances against him with the aim of dispossessing him: the minting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> currency at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, the circulation of French money in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and James's failure to attend the court at Barcelona. When James came to Barcelona in 1342 he accused Peter of trying to imprison him and went home without his wife and children, whom Peter kept, regarding the vassalage as broken. In 1343 he was found guilty and stripped of his lands.</p><p>That year Peter invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> and defeated James's troops at Santa Ponsa, and the islands quickly submitted. In campaigns of 1343 and 1344 Peter also took the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>, and James surrendered in July 1344 on condition that his life and liberty be spared, keeping only the Lordship of Montpellier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Diplomatic mediation failed, as did raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> in 1344 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conflent">Conflent</a> in 1347. James then paid for an expedition with the dowry of his marriage to Violante of Vilaragut, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a>, and by selling the Lordship of Montpellier to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, using the first instalment of the price to raise a fleet and hire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. With a fleet of 16 ships, among them eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, and about 3,000 infantry and 400 horsemen, he landed at Cap de Formentor and the Port de Pollença on 11 October 1349. Meeting resistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, he moved inland, reaching Porreres on the 22nd and Llucmajor on the 24th.</p><p>The defence was led by Peter's governor, Gilabert de Centelles, with reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> brought by Riambau de Corbera. The fighting lasted all morning and ended in the destruction of the Majorcan army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>James III was killed, and his son James IV was taken prisoner. James III's illegitimate brother, Pagà de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a>, was among the many others killed, and Queen Violante and her sister Elisabet were captured after the fighting. Peter IV kept Majorca, and the independent Kingdom of Majorca came to an end.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Llucmajor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Peter IV of Aragon seized the kingdom in 1343-1344, and James III was killed at <b>Llucmajor</b> in 1349 trying to retake it.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
